From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 09:57:02 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 31/57] hjviewer: same-account login: the PC takes a proof by
 its account's trust list

A Windows PC bound to an account lets the account's devices in without its
password (design v3 3-5, 3-6, 4-4 to 4-7). Thin hooks in the shared files, the
rest in HJViewer's:

- connection.rs: the Hash carries hjv_nonce, 32 random bytes in an account
  build's installed service and empty elsewhere, as 1.5.1 sends it. After the
  click approval branch a LoginRequest with a proof goes to
  connection/hjv_account.rs, which reads the latched self.lr: only where the
  permanent password is taken, past check_failure, on a secured channel, for
  a binding of this PC that is not switched off, the proof's signature checked
  against this PC's own ID, key and nonce before the cached list (active,
  within its life from its receipt, the key, its kinds, the controller's ID).
  Accepted, it goes on as the right permanent password does (2FA, the
  terminal's Windows login and the temporary password's rotation all in
  send_logon_response_and_keep_alive), the connection manager naming the
  device from the signed list and the masked owner. Refused, it answers
  "Empty Password" when no password came and leaves the login to the old
  branches; a refusal is not a failed attempt.
- rendezvous_mediator.rs: the service starts its sync task next to
  hbbs_http's.
- windows.rs: uninstall_me forgets HKLM\SOFTWARE\HJViewer\Account first,
  and adds `reg delete` of that key to the uninstaller's batch, which runs
  elevated once the service stopped: the Start menu's "Uninstall" runs
  --uninstall unelevated, where the first attempt gets access denied, and a
  PC installed again would take the earlier owner's proofs.
- hjviewer_account/pc.rs: the sync task. Every second it takes the UI's
  Config2 requests from memory: an unbind first, counting as undoing the bind
  request in hand (seen in the same second, or taken out of the options by
  the logout while hjv-api had it), then the bind request, the off switch and
  the knock. A sync's request runs in a task of its own, one at a time, so an
  unbind or the off switch takes effect within the second however slowly
  hjv-api answers. Requests are the relay envelope signed with the device
  key, signed again with hjv-api's clock after a clock answer, through one
  strict client; a bound PC asks every clamp(refresh, 5 s, 300 s) +-20%,
  backs off from 30 s to 10 min, asks at once after a resume, and a proof the
  list cannot decide waits up to 1.5 s for a sync (one at a time, none within
  10 s of the last one proofs waited for, so a phone that logged in just
  after a periodic sync gets in at once). The sync that is due goes before a
  bind request, which leaves it where it was: bind requests written every
  second hold off neither a revocation nor the list.
  Only a signed list changes anything. A bind request writes Binding, At,
  Owner and LoginAt only when the list confirms that binding, the account's
  last login on this PC is at most ten minutes older than the list and later
  than this binding's last release here, and the state is active or disabled;
  the answer to a bind request withdrawn while it was out (a logout took it
  out of the options, or a new login's request replaced it) binds nothing. A
  signed revoked drops the binding. The off switch moves to HKLM Off, the
  binding's latest login the PC knows of (LoginAt, or the bind request in
  hand for it, at most 5 min past hjv-api's clock), which only a later login
  clears. An unbind that drops this PC's binding, or undoes the bind request
  in hand, records UnboundBinding and UnboundLoginAt, its login_at held to
  what the PC knows of that binding's logins; it leaves another binding's
  record while that one guards the ten minutes after its login, and any
  other unbind changes nothing. Only a bind request (or an unbind undoing
  the bind request in hand) makes the key; everything else writes into an
  existing key or skips, so a key the uninstaller removed stays removed. The
  list bytes are kept in HJViewer_trust.json, vouched for by ListHash; Recv,
  KidFloor and LastState are in the key, and Use keeps today's and
  yesterday's use counts per key (UTC) for the report. Elsewhere than Windows
  the key is the 0600 file HJViewer_binding in the service's config folder.
- hjviewer_account/mod.rs: report_name gives only the design's seven refusal
  names, the ones the sync reports; forget_pc_binding is Windows-only and
  refresh_now the PC's own; korean(), the app's language as lang.rs picks
  it, names the device in the connection manager in Korean or English.

A build without HJV_SERVER, HJV_LOGIN_SEAL_PUBKEY or HJV_TRUST_PUBKEYS sends no
nonce and starts no task, as 1.5.1.

Tests: the bind, unbind and off rules on an in-memory key (a stale login,
another binding, a replayed request, a login undone before its bind came
through, again on a bound PC, a made-up login_at, a second made-up unbind, a
made-up bind request undone at once, the off switch with a login's bind
request in hand, the key removed under a running service), the Linux file's
mode, the list cache and its hash, the use counts across a restart and UTC
midnight, the sync request against the shared vectors, when a proof asks for
a sync, the settings line, no nonce and no task without the build variables,
and the sync task against an in-memory hjv-api on a paused clock: a login
binds, and one while the administrator's switch is off binds paused; the off
switch and an unbind act within the second while a request hangs; a login and
its logout found together, or a logout while the bind's request is out (also
while another binding's record guards), leave the PC unbound; a clock answer
is signed again; proofs share one sync and get none within ten seconds of it,
but one of their own after a periodic sync; a proof goes through the task (a
phone that logged in just now gets in, the next report counts uses and
refusals, a strict list needs hjv-api); bind requests every second do not
hold off the periodic sync; a stale bind request of this PC's binding leaves
its settings line; a signed revoked unbinds and one report follows; the
uninstaller's elevated batch deletes the binding key.

Known limits: a login undone before its bind came through is not recorded
while the PC's record of another binding still guards it (a login of that
one within the last 15 minutes), nor when the service found the login and its
logout in the same second; that login's bind request, put back while hjv-api
still has the login (a logout that never reached it), can then still bind
the PC. A connection the PC let in by a proof stays open when its account key
is revoked, the PC leaves the list, the switch is turned off or the list
expires: the PC checks the list only when it lets a connection in. Whoever
revokes ends such a connection in the PC's connection window, or connects to
the PC from their own device with the PC's password and uses "Restart remote
device", or restarts the PC (design v3 1-7, decided for 1.6.0: U1-d).

(Design v3, work package C2: patch 0031.)

